          "changeMeaning": "Displays a tetrahedral arrangement with four bonding domains, establishing the idealized electron-domain geometry.",
          "narration": "When the steric number is four, the electron-domain geometry is tetrahedral. In a tetrahedral arrangement, the four electron domains point toward the corners of a tetrahedron, with ideal bond angles of 109.5 degrees. This is the electron-domain geometry. But if one of those domains is a lone pair instead of a bonding pair, the molecular geometry changes."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip3_lone_pair_effect",
          "audioClip": "vsepr_lone_pair_repulsion",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "water_molecule_demo",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "water_oxygen_label",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "bond_angle_display",
              "enabled": true
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Demonstrates how lone pairs affect molecular shape by showing water as a bent molecule with reduced bond angle compared to tetrahedral ideal.",
          "narration": "Lone pairs occupy more space than bonding pairs. This is because a lone pair is attracted to only one nucleus, while a bonding pair is shared between two nuclei and is held more tightly. As a result, lone pairs repel other electron pairs more strongly. In water, oxygen has a steric number of four: two bonding pairs and two lone pairs. The two lone pairs occupy more angular space, pushing the bonding pairs closer together. This reduces the bond angle from the ideal 109.5 degrees to about 104.5 degrees, creating a bent molecular geometry."
        }
      ]
