          "changeMeaning": "Presents all five electron-domain geometries with their steric numbers and ideal bond angles for reference.",
          "narration": "There are five electron-domain geometries, each defined by its steric number. When the steric number is two, the geometry is linear with a 180-degree bond angle. When it is three, the geometry is trigonal planar with 120-degree angles. We have already discussed tetrahedral at steric number four with 109.5-degree angles. When the steric number is five, the geometry is trigonal bipyramidal: three domains in an equatorial plane at 120 degrees from each other, and two domains on the top and bottom at 180 degrees along the vertical axis. Finally, when the steric number is six, the geometry is octahedral, with six domains pointing toward the corners of an octahedron and all adjacent angles equal to 90 degrees."
        }
      ]
