          "changeMeaning": "Displays three molecules with tetrahedral electron-domain geometry but different molecular shapes due to varying numbers of lone pairs, illustrating the effect of lone-pair repulsion on bond angles.",
          "narration": "Let's apply VSEPR theory to real molecules. Methane, CH\u2084, has carbon with four bonding pairs and no lone pairs. Its steric number is four, giving a tetrahedral electron-domain geometry. Since all four domains are bonding pairs, the molecular geometry is also tetrahedral with bond angles of 109.5 degrees. Ammonia, NH\u2083, has nitrogen with three bonding pairs and one lone pair, also a steric number of four. The electron-domain geometry is tetrahedral, but the molecular geometry is trigonal pyramidal because one domain is a lone pair. The lone pair repels the bonding pairs more strongly, compressing the H-N-H bond angle to about 107 degrees. Finally, water, H\u2082O, has oxygen with two bonding pairs and two lone pairs, steric number four. The electron-domain geometry is tetrahedral, but with two lone pairs, the molecular geometry is bent. The two lone pairs occupy considerable space, pushing the bonding pairs closer and reducing the bond angle to 104.5 degrees."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip6_higher_steric_numbers",
