            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Introduces higher-steric-number molecules and demonstrates how VSEPR correctly predicts their shapes, including the square planar geometry of XeF\u2084 resulting from strategic lone-pair placement.",
          "narration": "For molecules with higher steric numbers, VSEPR predictions also match observations. Phosphorus pentafluoride, PF\u2085, has steric number five with five bonding pairs and no lone pairs. Its geometry is trigonal bipyramidal, with three fluorines in an equatorial plane and two in axial positions. Sulfur hexafluoride, SF\u2086, has steric number six with six bonding pairs and no lone pairs, yielding an octahedral geometry with all F-S-F angles at 90 degrees. Xenon tetrafluoride, XeF\u2084, is more interesting: xenon has six electron domains\u2014four bonding pairs to fluorine and two lone pairs\u2014giving a steric number of six and octahedral electron-domain geometry. However, the two lone pairs occupy opposite positions to minimize repulsion. The four fluorines end up in a square planar arrangement. This demonstrates that molecular geometry depends not just on steric number, but also on which domains are lone pairs."
        }
